  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nFor the start of this extended period, model agreement see the\nupper-level high pressure system stay situated across the Western\nUnited States. For the Panhandles, the positioning of the high\nputs us in a sort of mixed bag situation where we stay hot and\nrelatively dry, but have enough a mean wind to give storms the\nability to move in from mountains of Eastern New Mexico. The\nexception for this pattern will be seen this Sunday, however, as\nlow-level southerly flow looks to bring in drier air result in\nhotter and drier conditions being more prominent for the day. This\nflow will also lead to breezier conditions at the surface, which\ncan then lead to some elevated fire weather concerns during the\nafternoon period. However moving into next week, does see the\nchances of storms return once again across the Western Panhandles\nthanks to flow at the mid-level returning to a more westerly\ndirection. These chances do look to further grow and spread\ntowards the end of next week, with many models actually seeing the\nhigh start to weaken and become disorganized across the Southern\nUntied States. Either way, look for temperatures to keep hot with\n90s to triple-digits likely every day.",
